            <button class="node" type="button" data-q="tl" style="left:25%;top:25%" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false">GTM strategy</button>
            <button class="node" type="button" data-q="tr" style="left:75%;top:25%" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false">RevOps &amp; BizOps</button>
            <button class="node" type="button" data-q="bl" style="left:25%;top:75%" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false">GTM execution</button>
            <button class="node" type="button" data-q="br" style="left:75%;top:75%" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false">Systems &amp; automation</button>

            <div id="pins"></div>

            <div class="panel panel-y" id="panel-y" role="region" aria-label="Strategy to Execution">
              <button class="panel-close" type="button" aria-label="Close"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 12 12" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 1l10 10M11 1L1 11" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg></button>
              <div class="inner">
                <span class="poles mono">Strategy / Execution</span>
                <p>Strategy is the top half. Deciding what to change and why. Which accounts, which processes, which systems, in what order. Execution is the bottom half. Doing the work. Building the tool, writing the automation, fixing the process, and handing it off so it runs without me. Most consultants do one or the other. I do both, so the plan and the build come from the same person.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="panel panel-x" id="panel-x" role="region" aria-label="Go-to-market to Operations">
              <button class="panel-close" type="button" aria-label="Close"><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 12 12" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 1l10 10M11 1L1 11" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg></button>
              <div class="inner">
                <span class="poles mono">GTM / Operations</span>
                <p>Go-to-market is the left half. Anything that finds a buyer and moves them forward. Targeting, campaigns, content, paid media, sales follow-up. Operations is the right half. Anything that runs underneath. The CRM and its data, reporting, the systems that connect teams, automation and AI. Most problems sit on the line between the two. A pipeline problem is usually a process or tooling problem underneath, so I work both sides.</p>
